## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test across distributed caching and possible duplicate deliveries?
- How would you implement a contract test to ensure the idempotent endpoint returns identical payload across retries?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates expertise in designing comprehensive test strategies for complex multi-tenant data protection systems, particularly focusing on dynamic policy enforcement and reliability under various failure scenarios—essential for compliance-heavy enterprise environments.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-scoped field-level masking and policy data freshness
- Race conditions during real-time policy modifications
- Administrative rollback scenarios and data leakage risk assessment
- Cache interaction patterns and tenant churn performance optimization
- Contract testing, chaos engineering, and end-to-end resilience validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo example: contract test snippet using Pact-like syntax
const { pact } = require('pact-lang');

// Verify masked fields respect tenant-specific policies
pact({
  uponReceiving: 'request for customer data',
  withRequest: {
    method: 'GET',
    path: '/api/customers/123',
    headers: { 'X-Tenant-ID': 'tenant-A' }
  },
  willRespondWith: {
    status: 200,
    body: {
      id: '123',
      name: 'John Doe',
      // Sensitive field properly masked based on tenant policy
      ssn: '***-**-****'
    }
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for a WebSocket-based notification service require understanding ordering, deduplication, and resilience under real-time load. This angle explores concurrency, per-tenant quotas, and failure modes not deeply exercised by REST checks.

## Key Concepts

- WebSocket delivery guarantees: in-order messages, at-least-once delivery
- Per-tenant quotas and burst handling
- Backpressure, buffering, and flow control
- Reconnect logic and session resumption
- Fault injection and partial upstream outages

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test skeleton using ws library
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/notifications');
ws.on('open', () => ws.send(JSON.stringify({type:'subscribe', tenant:'t1'})));
ws.on('message', (data) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(data);
  // verify seqId monotonic per tenant and dedupe
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to 10k tenants with isolated streams?
- How would you validate behavior if the WebSocket layer fails and you must fall back to long-polling?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests across orchestration layers are common in real systems and subtle with async flows.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end orchestration
- Idempotency keys
- Eventual consistency
- Distributed tracing
- Failure injection

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudo-test using jest-like framework
test('end-to-end order orchestration', async () => {
  const key = generateIdempotencyKey();
  const res = await POST('/orders', { itemId, qty, idempotencyKey: key });
  expect(res.status).toBe(202);
  // then poll status until CONFIRMED or timeout
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify idempotent behavior under retry storms?
- What metrics ensure SLA for end-to-end latency?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end API testing in an event-driven pipeline, focusing on idempotency, schema versioning across tenants, and concurrency safety — realistic in multi-tenant ingestion workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent ingestion and deduplication across HTTP and Kafka
- Tenant-scoped schema versions and overrides
- End-to-end visibility with distributed tracing
- Race conditions and replay handling in high concurrency
- Tooling: Testcontainers, Pact, k6, Postman

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: idempotent ingestion using idempotency-key header
async function testIdempotentIngest(client) {
  const payload = {tenant: 't1', version: 2, data: {a:1}};
  const key = 'abc-123';
  await client.post('/ingest', payload, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  await client.post('/ingest', payload, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  // verify only single consume
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect exactly-once vs at-least-once processing in the consumer?
- How would you test schema evolution across tenants without breaking data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests expertise in distributed API reliability, multi-region data consistency, and chaos engineering practices for production-like environments.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end tests across regions
- Idempotency keys, deduplication, exactly-once vs at-least-once
- Kafka semantics: offsets, retries, replay protection
- Chaos engineering: fault injection, partitions, partial outages
- Observability: traces, metrics, logs, service mesh signals

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test harness for idempotent order creation and event replay
async function testIdempotentPostOrder(orderPayload) {
  const id = await postOrder(orderPayload);
  // retry with same payload
  const id2 = await postOrder(orderPayload);
  if (id !== id2) throw new Error('Idempotency key not preserved');
  const state = await getOrder(id);
  if (!state || state.status !== 'CONFIRMED') throw new Error('Order not final');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate idempotency across retries and regions in CI/CD?
- How would you test replay protection for downstream events across services and ensure no drift across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical API testing skills for real-world payments: idempotency, webhooks, and concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotent operations and Idempotency-Key
- External payment provider mocking and webhook handling
- Retry/backoff strategies and race-condition testing
- End-to-end flow verification with minimal flakiness

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test for idempotent checkout
test('idempotent checkout creates single order', async () => {
  const key = 'abc123';
  const res1 = await POST('/checkout', {items:[1,2,3]}, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  const res2 = await POST('/checkout', {items:[1,2,3]}, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  expect(res1.status).toBe(200);
  expect(res2.status).toBe(200);
  expect(res2.body.id).toBe(res1.body.id);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt tests if the payment provider supports idempotent retries with in-flight charges?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect duplicate charges or webhook delays?
